"""Weak vegan arguments in English, keyed by the Portuguese phrase in tools/ruins.py. Sources in Portuguese are marked."""

_VA = ('13 pro-vegan arguments of dubious quality (in Portuguese)', 'https://veganagente.com.br/argumentos-veganos-ruins/')

TIPOS = {
    'indireto': ('Borrowed reason', 'They talk about health, hunger or the planet instead of the animals. When the borrowed reason falls, the motivation falls with it.'),
    'impreciso': ('Exaggeration or attack', 'They claim more than the evidence supports, or attack people instead of discussing what they do.'),
    'criterio': ('Wrong criterion', 'They ground the defense of animals in faith, affection or hierarchy, not in sentience.'),
    'tatica': ('Objection in disguise', 'They look like concern for strategy or for the animals, but become a ready excuse to change nothing.'),
    'fato': ('A fact with no moral weight', 'They bring up a biological curiosity as if it settled the question. What matters is what happens to the animals.'),
    'ciencia': ('Weak or false science', 'They make claims about the body or nutrition that the evidence does not support. When they fall, they take the speaker\'s credibility with them.'),
    'pureza': ('Purity and invented rules', 'They create demands veganism does not make and push away people who might join, without helping a single animal.'),
    'concessao': ('A concession that empties the point', 'Said to seem flexible or avoid conflict, they turn a question about victims into a personal preference.'),
    'pauta': ('The cause as a tool', 'They use the animal movement for another purpose, or pretend two goals never conflict.'),
}

RUINS = {
 'Ser vegano vai te deixar mais saudável.': dict(frase='Going vegan will make you healthier.',
   por=["It's imprecise: any badly planned diet carries risks.", "And even if it were true, it speaks to the eater's choice, not to the animals. Someone who stops eating animals for their health will drop the position the day they find evidence, real or apparent, that they can be healthy eating animals. Technology may even make those products safer than they are today."],
   lugar='A well-planned vegan diet is viable. That shows the duty not to harm animals can be met, but the duty comes from them, not from your health.'),
 'O veganismo resolveria a fome no mundo.': dict(frase='Veganism would end world hunger.',
   por=['It ignores that hunger is largely a problem of distribution and political will, not just of farm output.', 'It also puts a human benefit in the place that should belong to the animal itself.'],
   lugar='Harming sentient beings without necessity is unjust, with or without any effect on hunger.'),
 'Vire vegano pelo planeta.': dict(frase='Go vegan for the planet.',
   por=["The problem isn't that the environmental argument is false. It's that it is fragile exactly where it most needs to be solid: if the reason to reject animal farming is its environmental impact, cleaner animal farming would solve the problem without solving anything for the animals.", 'The industry already invests in "sustainable" versions that hollow out this argument from the inside. "Sustainable meat" and "conscious farming" brands sell exactly that promise: the animal is still property and is still killed.', 'This argument also reinforces the environmentalist view, in which entities like ecosystems matter in themselves, rather than the sentience-centered view.'],
   lugar='The environment matters as a means: preserving or changing an ecosystem makes moral sense insofar as it improves the lives of real sentient beings.',
   nota="The guide doesn't ask you to drop your environmental concern. It asks you not to mix criteria and, when they conflict, to give sentient beings priority."),
 'Quem come carne é psicopata.': dict(frase='Meat eaters are psychopaths.',
   por=["It's inaccurate and counterproductive. Most of these people have genuine emotional bonds.", "The problem isn't a lack of emotion. It's an ethical inconsistency that extends respect only to a selected group."],
   lugar='Point out the inconsistency: the person already cares about some animals, and nothing relevant separates those animals from the ones they eat.'),
 'Deus não fez os animais para os humanos.': dict(frase="God didn't make animals for humans.",
   por=["Religious arguments of this kind depend on belief, not on reasons anyone can share, so they don't persuade people of a different faith.", 'They also open the door for any opposite belief to be equally valid, since none can be checked.'],
   lugar='Use a reason anyone can evaluate, whatever their faith: the animal can be harmed, and that harm needs a justification.'),
 'Devemos amar os animais.': dict(frase='We should love animals.',
   por=["It confuses affection with respect. No one is obliged to love humans or animals, but no one has the right to harm them for that reason.", 'The same goes for the variants "I\'m vegan because I love animals", "animals are my friends, and I don\'t eat my friends" and "veganism is love and mercy". You don\'t need to be friends with everyone in another country to know they have rights.', 'These phrases also make veganism look like pure emotion, when it has a rational foundation.', 'The question "if you love some, why do you eat others?" has the same problem: it assumes an affection not everyone has, and it loses its force as soon as the person answers that they don\'t love any animal.'],
   lugar="You don't have to like cows to stop paying for their deaths. The question is justice, not affection. When someone does love an animal, it's worth pointing out the inconsistency, but rest the conclusion on what holds for everyone: what relevant difference is there between a dog and a pig, if both can suffer?",
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'Os humanos vêm primeiro.': dict(frase='Humans come first.',
   por=["As a general principle, it is plain speciesism, whether applied by vegans or non-vegans.", "Rejecting speciesism means giving similar weight to similar harms, whatever the species. The exception is only the very rare case of a real, unavoidable conflict between a human life and an animal life, which is nothing like the everyday habit of exploiting animals where viable alternatives exist."],
   lugar='Similar harms deserve similar weight. Causes can have different priorities because of the number of victims, neglect and tractability, not because of species.'),
 'Comparar com escravidão afasta as pessoas.': dict(frase='Comparing it to slavery pushes people away.',
   por=['It can look like a strategic concern. The test is simple: if it were shown that the comparison is more effective at improving the situation of animals, would you start supporting it? If the answer is no, the objection was always moral, just dressed up as tactics.', "There's nothing wrong with having a moral objection to a tactic. The problem is disguising it, because that prevents the real disagreement from being discussed.", 'Concern about offense deserves to be taken seriously, but it has to be applied consistently. Being compared to a nonhuman animal is only offensive to someone who already assumes animals are worth less.'],
   lugar="Say what your real objection is. If it's moral, defend it as moral. If it's strategic, show the evidence."),
 'Não mostre as imagens, é chocante demais.': dict(frase="Don't show the footage; it's too shocking.",
   por=['At some point this caution becomes an inverted priority: it treats the discomfort of the viewer as more important than the suffering of those being filmed.', 'The gap between a statistic and a concrete image is exactly what switches empathy on or off. Always hiding that reality reinforces the mechanism that sustains indifference.'],
   lugar='Ask which approach reduces the most suffering, not which is most comfortable to defend.'),
 'Essa lei parcial é injusta com os outros animais.': dict(frase='This partial law is unfair to the other animals.',
   por=["It sounds like an even stronger commitment, but it proves too much: since it's impossible to ban every form of exploitation at once, someone who cares about outcomes would prefer a real reduction in suffering to none.", "The same person would hardly oppose a law that reduced part of some human suffering because it didn't solve every human problem at once."],
   lugar='Judge each law by what it causes over time. The abolition-versus-reform debate is real, but it is empirical and needs evidence case by case.'),
 'Carne cultivada não é vegana, pois é de origem animal.': dict(frase="Cultivated meat isn't vegan, because it comes from animals.",
   por=['The definitional objection confuses the letter of the commitment with its spirit: veganism is not a closed list of forbidden ingredients, it is a commitment to ending unnecessary harm to sentient beings.', 'The "it\'s lab food" variant is the naturalness fallacy. There is nothing more natural about an industrial dairy cow than about a protein grown in a bioreactor. The relevant difference is which process causes more suffering.', 'Rejecting the technology because it feels like a symbolic surrender sacrifices the result for a purity that benefits no real animal.'],
   lugar="There's a real risk that the technology becomes a badge of \"conscience\" without replacing animal farming. That justifies vigilance, not outright rejection."),
 'Ser vegano exige ser anticapitalista.': dict(frase='Being vegan requires being anti-capitalist.',
   por=["When someone says this without saying, just as firmly, that anti-capitalists should be vegan, the real goal isn't animal welfare. It's recruiting supporters for another cause from inside the vegan movement.", "The same goes for any cause: it's true that humans deserve consideration, but that doesn't license demanding only of animal advocates that they adopt other specific causes."],
   lugar='Defend your political position as a political position, with its own arguments, without presenting it as a condition for defending animals.'),
 'Carne mais cara é ruim para o povo.': dict(frase='More expensive meat is bad for ordinary people.',
   por=['When the price of animal products rises, the drop in consumption is usually treated only as sad news, and not also as what it is: fewer animals killed.', 'The obvious demand of an animal advocate would not be to keep prices low, but for every form of animal exploitation to become more expensive to the same degree, so that consumption does not shift to eggs and milk.', 'Improving the lives of poor populations is a legitimate goal. The mistake is pretending there is never tension with animal suffering, always leaving the burden on those who have no voice to complain.'],
   lugar='Recognize when two legitimate goals conflict and discuss how to resolve it, instead of pretending the conflict does not exist.'),
 'Gatos não podem ser veganos.': dict(frase="Cats can't be vegan.",
   por=['Many vegans repeat this as if it were established fact, but the available evidence points the other way. In a study of 1,369 cats published in PLOS ONE in 2023, the 127 fed a vegan diet tended to be as healthy or healthier on all seven general indicators, and a 2021 study of more than a thousand cats in Canada and the US also found no harm. A vegan cat food tested at the Federal University of Lavras, in Brazil, met the cats\' needs on the measures assessed.',
        'What a cat needs is nutrients, not meat. Taurine, preformed vitamin A and arachidonic acid can be supplied synthetically, and the taurine in ordinary cat food is already synthetic. No extruded dry food is "natural".',
        "If current foods aren't enough for some cat, that's a food engineering problem to solve, not an impossibility. Precision fermentation and cultivated meat already produce ingredients without killing animals: the UK approved cultivated meat in pet food in 2024.",
        'The chickens, cattle and fish that become cat food want to live too, and there are many of them. Saying cats can\'t be vegan closes the door on saving those animals before even trying.'],
   lugar='The evidence suggests cats can live well on complete vegan foods, carefully formulated and with veterinary follow-up. Where that still falls short, the answer is to develop better foods, not to give up on the animals that become cat food.',
   nota='What the studies support is "may be adequate", not "proven to be better": the data come from owner reports, most differences were not statistically significant, and the Lavras study used only 18 cats. An unformulated home-made diet is risky, with or without meat, and a cat may refuse a particular food.',
   fontes=[('Can cats be vegan? What science says (in Portuguese)', 'https://vegpedia.com/2026/04/10/gatos-podem-ser-veganos-o-que-a-ciencia-diz/'), ('Vegan versus meat-based cat food: guardian-reported health outcomes in 1,369 cats', 'https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0284132'), ('A cross-sectional study of owner-reported health in Canadian and American cats fed meat- and plant-based diets', 'https://doi.org/10.1186/s12917-021-02754-8'), ('The Impact of Vegan Diets on Indicators of Health in Dogs and Cats: A Systematic Review', None), ('Meatly gets UK approval for use of cultivated meat in pet food', 'https://www.foodnavigator.com/Article/2024/07/17/Meatly-gets-UK-approval-for-use-of-cultivated-meat-in-pet-food/')]),
 'Não existe ex-vegano.': dict(frase="There's no such thing as an ex-vegan.",
   por=['Being vegan is an ethical position held up by arguments and repeated choices, not a genetic trait. Any such position can be abandoned. There are ex-abolitionists, ex-feminists and ex-pacifists for the same reason.', "The phrase confuses whether an argument is valid with how psychologically stable the people who accepted it are. A position can be more desirable without being quit-proof.", 'The variant "whoever quit was never really vegan" is the "no true Scotsman" fallacy: it redefines the category after the fact to exclude the counterexample. It also assumes access to someone\'s inner conviction, which no one has.'],
   lugar="Ex-vegans exist, but that says nothing in favor of the position they went back to. Someone ceasing to be an abolitionist doesn't make slavery acceptable.",
   nota="There are, indeed, people who call themselves vegan while supporting exploitation, almost always because they don't know what the word means. That's different from denying that someone was vegan and changed.",
   fontes=[('Is it possible to be an ex-vegan? (in Portuguese)', 'https://vegpedia.com/2026/09/09/ex-vegano/')]),
 'Usar força para proteger animais é sempre errado.': dict(frase='Using force to protect animals is always wrong.',
   por=['The question is more open than that certainty suggests. Absolute pacifism trips over its own logic: refusing to stop a greater harm, in practice, lets it happen. And we accept forceful intervention for humans who cannot defend themselves, such as a child being attacked.', '"It turns the public away" has been repeated for decades with little solid evidence. Concern for animal welfare grew during the period when direct action groups were already operating, which proves nothing in either direction.', 'The best-documented cost is a different one: harsher laws against activism, such as the US Animal Enterprise Terrorism Act and the "ag-gag" laws that criminalize filming farms. On the other side of the scale are rescues and exposés that saved animals. No one has shown which side weighs more.', 'The mirror-image error is just as bad: saying force works. Reviews of social movements suggest confrontational tactics have ambiguous effects, and actions meant to be non-lethal have had tragic consequences for the animals themselves.', 'There is a parallel with the paradox of tolerance, described by the philosopher Karl Popper in 1945: unlimited tolerance, which tolerates even those who want to destroy it, ends up disappearing. In a similar way, refusing any force whatsoever, in any situation, leaves the way open for the violence already being done to animals: what could be called a paradox of nonviolence. The parallel has limits. Popper held that the intolerant should first be answered with arguments, and accepted force only against those who refuse debate and turn to violence. What the comparison shows is that absolute pacifism also has a cost, not that force is justified in each case.'],
   lugar='Separate the cases, because rescue, property damage and violence against people are different things, and ask what each one actually produces, with evidence instead of conviction.',
   nota="This guide does not recommend any violent or illegal action. The point is the quality of the argument: violence against people finds very little support even among theorists sympathetic to the discussion, and the philosophical question about the other cases remains open.",
   fontes=[('Defense by proxy: may we use force to protect animals? (in Portuguese)', 'https://vegpedia.com/2026/09/02/uso-da-forca-para-proteger-os-animais/'), ('Three objections to the use of violence in defense of nonhuman animals and why they fail (in Portuguese)', 'https://dialnet.unirioja.es/servlet/articulo?codigo=10003509'), ('Gaps in Logic, Lapses in Politics: Rights and Abolitionism in Joan Dunayer’s Speciesism', 'https://web.archive.org/web/20251002184604/https://drstevebest.org/GapsInLogic.htm'), ('Paradox of tolerance', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradox_of_tolerance'), ('Summary of Evidence for Foundational Questions in Effective Animal Advocacy', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries')]),
 'Não é sua mãe, não é seu leite.': dict(frase="Not your mom, not your milk.",
   por=["It's an appeal to nature: it suggests milk from another species is wrong because nature didn't reserve it for us. Natural doesn't define right, and vegans point out this same mistake in \"it's natural to eat meat\".", 'The variant "humans are the only animals that keep drinking milk as adults" has the same flaw. Accurate or not, no other animal cooks, gets vaccinated or drinks oat milk, and none of that is wrong for that reason.', 'Another variant is "milk belongs to the calves". Where the milk "naturally" goes is a weak reason compared with what happens to the cow and the calf: the problem is the exploitation, not who the liquid belonged to.', 'The phrase also shifts the topic to the purity of the drinker. If someone proves milk is good for adults, it loses its force, and the animals are no better off.'],
   lugar='Talk about who pays the price: the cow has to be made pregnant to produce, the male calves are discarded and the cows are slaughtered when production drops.',
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'Ovo é menstruação de galinha.': dict(frase="Eggs are chicken periods.",
   por=["Even if the comparison were accurate, it says nothing moral. Being gross doesn't make something wrong, and not being gross wouldn't make it right. The listener can simply say they don't care.", 'The biology is also off: an egg results from ovulation, while menstruation is the shedding of the uterine lining. An argument that is easy to correct gives people a reason to dismiss everything else.', "The appeal to disgust swaps the victim for the eater's feelings. An egg involving no harm at all, like the rescued hen's in F6, would be a different discussion, precisely because the issue was never its biological origin."],
   lugar='Talk about what happens to the birds: the male chicks are discarded and the hens are slaughtered when production drops.'),
 'Sou vegano por compaixão a todos os seres vivos.': dict(frase="I'm vegan out of compassion for all living beings.",
   por=["Veganism's criterion isn't being alive, it's being sentient. Plants, fungi and single-celled organisms are alive, but there's no evidence they can be harmed in the sense that matters.", 'The phrase also hands people the "what about plants?" reply, since eating lettuce also kills a living being.'],
   lugar='Say "sentient beings": those who can feel and be harmed by what we do.',
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'O ser humano é biologicamente herbívoro.': dict(frase='Humans are biologically herbivores.',
   por=["It isn't an ethical argument. It suggests we should be vegan because we can't make use of meat, milk and eggs, not because exploiting animals is wrong.", 'It also has a weak scientific basis: the human body digests animal foods, and history records no epidemic of intolerance caused by them.', 'The variant "put a baby next to an apple and a rabbit: it plays with the rabbit and eats the apple" cites no study at all. It is an appeal to ignorance as well as an appeal to nature.'],
   lugar="Even if we were great at digesting meat, that wouldn't tell us whether it's right. We can live well without it, and that's the information that matters.",
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'Veganos são mais sexies e melhores de cama.': dict(frase='Vegans are sexier and better in bed.',
   por=["It takes the focus off the animals and appeals to the listener's self-interest.", 'It makes a scientific claim with no serious studies, ignores factors like stress and depression, and assumes every vegan diet is light.', 'It reinforces a thin body ideal, discriminating against fat people, and cheapens the debate.'],
   lugar="Talk about who is harmed. Being vegan isn't a beauty program.",
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'Alimento que pode conter traços de leite não é vegano.': dict(frase="Food that may contain traces of milk isn't vegan.",
   por=['"May contain traces" is a warning for people with allergies: the product went through machines that also process that ingredient. The ingredient isn\'t in the recipe.', "A trace isn't an ingredient. Anyone can avoid these products as a personal choice, but that doesn't make them non-vegan, and the rule only makes veganism harder for beginners."],
   lugar='Check whether there is an animal ingredient and whether the company carries out or sponsors testing or other exploitation.',
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'Brócolis e espinafre têm mais proteína que carne.': dict(frase='Broccoli and spinach have more protein than meat.',
   por=['The confusion comes from charts that count protein per calorie, not per weight. About half of spinach\'s calories come from protein, but it has very few calories: 100 g provides only about 3 g of protein.', 'Anyone who repeats this is refuted by a glance at a nutrition table, and their credibility goes with it. Worse, someone might rely on the idea when planning their own diet.'],
   lugar='Good plant sources exist: beans, lentils, chickpeas and soy. Always compare grams of protein per 100 g of food.',
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'Veganos não precisam suplementar B12.': dict(frase="Vegans don't need to supplement B12.",
   por=['It is irresponsible and can put people\'s health at risk. Calling supplementation "a pharmaceutical industry scam" is a conspiracy claim with no evidence.', '"Unwashed" foods are not a reliable source and can carry harmful microorganisms.', 'A vegan who gets sick from lack of B12 harms themselves and becomes ammunition against the movement.'],
   lugar="Anyone who doesn't eat animal products needs a reliable source of B12, such as a supplement or fortified food, ideally with professional guidance. Needing a supplement doesn't make the diet invalid.",
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'Vegano bom só come natural, orgânico e saudável.': dict(frase='A good vegan only eats natural, organic, healthy food.',
   por=["Nothing in vegan ethics requires that. The concern is not exploiting animals, whether dinner is an organic salad or French fries.", 'The argument creates a food code that doesn\'t exist, discourages people thinking of joining and can drive newcomers away.'],
   lugar="Taking care of your health is great, but it's a different conversation. To be vegan, it's enough not to exploit animals as far as possible.",
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'Veganismo é só pelos animais, o resto não importa.': dict(frase="Veganism is only about animals; nothing else matters.",
   por=["Veganism does focus on animal exploitation, but that's no license to treat other humans unfairly or with prejudice. Humans are sentient too, and the criterion applies to them.", 'Using the cause as an excuse for prejudice is inconsistent: it reproduces the moral exclusion the movement criticizes.'],
   lugar="Veganism has a focus, and prioritizing it is legitimate. That doesn't oblige anyone to embrace every cause (see {V12}), but it doesn't free anyone from respecting people either.",
   fontes=[_VA]),
 'Sou vegano, mas provo comida quando visito meus familiares.': dict(frase="I'm vegan, but I taste the food when I visit my family.",
   por=["If the reason not to eat animals is the animal, it doesn't change because the table belongs to your family. For whoever died, the occasion makes no difference.", 'The phrase also teaches the family that veganism is negotiable: a diet suspended out of politeness, not a position about victims. Anyone who insists has reason to think pressure will work.', 'This is not a demand for perfection. A mistake, an accident or a hard moment are something else, and veganism speaks of "as far as is possible and practicable". A family visit is uncomfortable, but it rarely makes refusing impossible.'],
   lugar='Decline kindly, without a sermon: "Thanks, I don\'t eat that, but I love being here." Bringing a dish to share usually makes the conversation easier.'),
 'Sou vegano, mas não acho que todo mundo deveria ser.': dict(frase="I'm vegan, but I don't think everyone should be.",
   por=['It treats as personal taste something that has a victim. No one says "I\'m against assaulting people, but I don\'t think everyone should be." If the harm is unjust when you cause it, it is also unjust when someone else does.', "It's usually said to avoid sounding arrogant, but that's a matter of tone. You can be kind and not judge the person without giving up the conclusion.", "There are legitimate distinctions the phrase erases: someone with no alternative isn't in the same situation as someone who has one, and saying something is wrong is not the same as arguing it should be banned now."],
   lugar="Anyone who can avoid harming animals has a reason to. I know change takes time, and I'm here to talk, not to judge you."),
 'Ser vegano é muito caro.': dict(frase='Being vegan is very expensive.',
   por=['Coming from a vegan, the phrase confirms one of the myths most used against veganism, and pushes away people who might join.', 'It usually generalizes from specialty products, like vegan cheeses and meats. Beans, rice, grains and vegetables are among the cheapest foods.', "The access barrier is real for some people, and it's worth acknowledging. But that's different from saying veganism is expensive by nature."],
   lugar="Processed substitutes can be expensive, but the basis of a vegan diet doesn't have to be. Start with beans, rice, grains and vegetables."),
 'Gorilas, touros e cavalos ficam fortes comendo só plantas.': dict(frase='Gorillas, bulls and horses get strong eating only plants.',
   por=["It's a false analogy. Cattle digest grass with the help of microbes in the rumen, and horses ferment fiber in the large intestine; the microbes turn fiber and other compounds into nutrients, including protein. Gorillas spend much of the day eating large volumes of leaves and have a gut adapted to ferment them. The human digestive system doesn't work like that.", "So a herbivore's strength says nothing about what the human body needs. Anyone who knows the subject takes the argument apart in one sentence, and the speaker's credibility goes with it.", "It's also an appeal to nature: even if the analogy held, what other animals do doesn't decide what is right for us to do."],
   lugar='Use evidence about humans: well-planned plant-based diets provide the protein we need, and there are top athletes who don\'t eat animals. And remember that the main question is moral, not nutritional.'),
 'Nenhum animal adulto bebe leite.': dict(frase='No adult animal drinks milk.',
   por=["It's an appeal to nature. We do many things no other animal does, like cooking, and that doesn't make them wrong; other animals not drinking milk doesn't make milk wrong either.", "And it leaves out what matters: what milk production does to cows and calves."],
   lugar='Talk about production: repeated pregnancies, separation of calves and slaughter of cows when production drops. That is what makes milk a moral problem.'),
 'A anemia atinge sobretudo quem come carne.': dict(frase='Anemia mostly affects meat eaters.',
   por=["It's a base-rate fallacy: since most people eat meat, most cases of almost anything occur among meat eaters. That doesn't show that meat causes anemia.", "And it hides something worth acknowledging: vegetarians and vegans tend to have lower iron stores."],
   lugar="Eating meat doesn't prevent anemia, which is common on every diet. What prevents it is planned eating, with iron sources, vitamin C at meals and tests when needed."),
 'Toda a B12 da carne vem de suplemento.': dict(frase='All the B12 in meat comes from supplements.',
   por=["It's an exaggeration. Farmed pigs and chickens get B12 in their feed, but in cattle B12 is produced by bacteria in the rumen from cobalt, which doesn't always need to be added.", 'The true point is more modest and enough: B12 comes from bacteria, and much of what reaches meat eaters went through supplements given to the animals.'],
   lugar='B12 is made by bacteria, not by animals. Taking a supplement just gets the same vitamin without the middleman.'),
 'Todas as entidades de nutrição do mundo aprovam a dieta vegana para qualquer pessoa.': dict(frase='Every nutrition body in the world approves vegan diets for everyone.',
   por=["It overstates the consensus. The US Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics and the British Dietetic Association say well-planned vegan diets can be appropriate at every stage of life. But the German Nutrition Society, in 2024, neither recommended nor advised against vegan diets for pregnant women, children and older adults, for lack of data, and European pediatric bodies are cautious.", 'Overstating it gives the other person an easy rebuttal, and the discussion becomes about the overstatement.', 'It also leaves out the conditions all these bodies set: planning, B12 and, for vulnerable groups, professional follow-up.'],
   lugar="Say who says what: bodies such as the US Academy and the British Dietetic Association accept well-planned vegan diets at every stage of life, with B12 and planning; others urge more caution for pregnancy and children. None says you need to eat animals."),
 'Médico não entende nada de nutrição.': dict(frase="Doctors don't know anything about nutrition.",
   por=["It's a generalization. Nutrition training in medical school is often short, but that doesn't mean no doctor understands the subject, and many specialize in it.", "It also dismisses the person's own doctor without examining the reason for the advice. Following your doctor isn't a fallacy; there may be a real clinical reason."],
   lugar='Ask the reason for the advice: which nutrient is missing and whether it can come from another source. If it is a general opinion, compare it with the positions of nutrition bodies and look for a dietitian who knows plant-based diets.'),
 'Quem come carne come mais soja do que o vegano.': dict(frase='Meat eaters eat more soy than vegans.',
   por=["It's true that about three quarters of the world's soy becomes animal feed, and that is a good argument about deforestation.", "But used against the fear of soy hormones, it's a non sequitur: meat eaters don't ingest the isoflavones in the feed the animal ate. The right answer to that fear is the studies in people, which show no effect on hormones."],
   lugar='For the fear of hormones, cite the studies in humans. For deforestation, yes, point out that most soy feeds animals.'),
 'A pecuária polui mais do que todos os carros, aviões e navios juntos.': dict(frase='Livestock pollutes more than all cars, planes and ships combined.',
   por=["The phrase comes from the 2006 FAO report, which compared livestock's 18% of emissions with about 13% for transport. But the accounting differed: for livestock it counted the whole chain, from feed to slaughter; for transport, only fuel combustion. One of the authors acknowledged the problem in 2010.", 'Repeating the comparison gives the other person an easy rebuttal, and the discussion becomes about the number.'],
   lugar='Use comparable figures: livestock accounts for between 12% and 14.5% of human emissions, according to the FAO, and animal-based foods for about 57% of food production emissions. That is already a lot, without a mistaken comparison.',
   fontes=[("Livestock's Long Shadow", 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livestock%27s_Long_Shadow')]),
 'A pecuária causa 51% das emissões de gases de efeito estufa do mundo.': dict(frase="Livestock causes 51% of the world's greenhouse gas emissions.",
   por=['The figure comes from a 2009 piece published by the Worldwatch Institute, which counted, for example, animal respiration, something international methodologies do not count as emissions. It became famous through the documentary *Cowspiracy*.', 'Researchers from several institutions, including authors of the FAO report, challenged the method. The most accepted estimates are between 12% and 14.5%.', 'Using the highest, contested figure costs credibility, and the correct argument, which is already strong, gets buried.'],
   lugar='Say that livestock accounts for between 12% and 14.5% of human emissions and about 40% of human-caused methane, together with the rest of agriculture. These are accepted figures, and they are enough.',
   fontes=[('Livestock and climate change: towards credible figures', 'https://newsarchive.ilri.org/archives/6239')]),
 'Testes em animais não servem para nada: 90% dos remédios falham.': dict(frase='Animal testing is useless: 90% of drugs fail.',
   por=["It's an exaggeration. About 90% of drug candidates fail in human trials, and species differences are one of the causes. But failures have many other causes, and the figure doesn't prove that tests have never helped.", 'Saying they are "useless" is refuted with one historical example, and the moral objection, which is the strongest, is pushed aside.'],
   lugar="Animal tests have serious limits and are no longer required for every drug in the US. But the main question is a different one: a benefit doesn't justify using those who cannot consent, and we don't accept that with humans."),
 'Carne sem tempero não tem gosto de nada; todo o sabor vem do tempero.': dict(frase='Unseasoned meat tastes of nothing; all the flavor comes from seasoning.',
   por=["It's an exaggeration. Meat has its own flavor, which comes from fat, from compounds like heme iron and from the reactions that happen when it browns.", "Meat lovers know this, and the phrase sounds like denying the obvious. The strongest argument doesn't need it."],
   lugar="Much of the flavor we associate with meat comes from preparation and seasoning, and that can be applied to plants. And even if meat tasted better, the pleasure of a meal wouldn't justify an animal's life."),
 'Na Índia quase todo mundo é vegano.': dict(frase='In India almost everyone is vegan.',
   por=['It confuses vegetarianism with veganism. About 39% of Indian adults say they are vegetarian, and milk consumption in the country is very high.', "The example is good for something else: showing that not eating meat isn't a habit of a white, wealthy elite. Exaggerating it weakens that point."],
   lugar='In India, about four in ten adults say they are vegetarian, and eight in ten restrict meat in some way. Refusing meat is old and diverse, and it does not belong to one social group.'),
 'Cada vegano salva 200 animais por ano.': dict(frase='Each vegan saves 200 animals a year.',
   por=["The number looks precise, but it's a simple division: total animals killed divided by the number of people. It ignores that production doesn't fall in the same proportion as demand, because prices drop and others buy part of what was not bought.", 'The figures in circulation vary widely depending on whether fish and small animals are counted, and anyone who asks "where does that come from?" finds little basis.'],
   lugar="Each purchase has a real average effect on production, less than one animal per animal not bought, but more than zero. Over a lifetime of thousands of meals, that adds up to many animals."),
 'Está provado que veganos são mais empáticos.': dict(frase="It's proven that vegans are more empathetic.",
   por=['The claim usually relies on a 2010 neuroimaging study of 60 people, which measured brain activation in response to images, not behavior. It is modest evidence, not proof.', 'It also sounds like moral superiority, which is exactly the stereotype that pushes people away (L1).'],
   lugar="Say that caring for animals doesn't reduce care for people: empathy isn't zero-sum, and many animal advocates also work for human causes."),
 'Hitler nem era vegetariano de verdade.': dict(frase="Hitler wasn't even really a vegetarian.",
   por=["Hitler's diet is debated by historians, and answering on historical grounds unintentionally accepts that it would matter to the discussion.", 'If proof appeared that he was vegetarian, the answer would collapse with it.'],
   lugar="Even if he was vegetarian, that would say nothing about veganism, just as his not smoking doesn't make cigarettes good. What matters are the arguments about the animals."),
 'Porcos são mais inteligentes que uma criança de três anos.': dict(frase='Pigs are smarter than a three-year-old child.',
   por=['The comparison is widely shared but rests on weak studies. The experiment in which pigs used a mirror to find food has not been replicated, and comparing the intelligence of species by a single measure has no solid basis.', "And there's a trap: using intelligence as the argument accepts the other person's criterion. If the pig deserves respect for being intelligent, the chicken and the fish, supposedly less intelligent, would deserve less."],
   lugar='Pigs have real cognitive and social abilities, but what matters is that they feel pain, fear and pleasure. That holds for the pig, the chicken and the fish, however intelligent they are.'),
 'A gorila Koko conversava em língua de sinais.': dict(frase='Koko the gorilla held conversations in sign language.',
   por=['Koko learned many signs, but the claim that she used a language, with grammar and new sentences, does not hold up: the interpretation depended heavily on her trainers.', 'The argument also suggests that animals matter for resembling us in language, which leaves out almost all exploited animals.'],
   lugar="Animal communication is rich, and pain can be measured without words, through facial expressions and behavior. You don't have to speak to suffer, just like a baby or a person who has lost speech."),
 'Todos os animais são mortos conscientes, sem atordoamento.': dict(frase='All animals are slaughtered fully conscious, without stunning.',
   por=["It's a generalization. In the European Union and in Brazil, the law requires stunning cattle, pigs and poultry before bleeding, and in the United States federal law requires it for mammals, though not for poultry. Most slaughter follows these rules, even with failures and exceptions.", 'Anyone who knows the industry takes the phrase apart on the spot, and the real problem, which is serious, hides behind the exaggeration.'],
   lugar="Say what the law allows and what fails: birds shackled conscious before stunning, a gas that pigs avoid, religious slaughter without stunning, fish almost always killed by suffocation. And even with the best stunning, the animal is still killed."),
 'Está provado que a carne não teve nenhum papel na evolução humana.': dict(frase="It's proven that meat played no role in human evolution.",
   por=["It overstates a real study. Barr and colleagues showed in 2022 that the apparent increase in evidence of meat-eating in the time of *Homo erectus* disappears once research effort is accounted for. That weakens one hypothesis, but it doesn't prove meat played no role.", 'Our ancestors hunted and ate animals, and that is well documented. Denying it overreaches in the opposite direction and gives the other person an easy rebuttal.'],
   lugar="The exact role of meat in evolution is debated, and cooking food, including plants, is another strong explanation. But the main point is different: what helped our ancestors survive doesn't tell us what to do today, with alternatives."),
 'Jesus era vegetariano.': dict(frase='Jesus was a vegetarian.',
   por=['The Gospels describe Jesus eating and offering fish, and the theories that he was vegetarian, for example through a link with the Essenes, have no solid historical basis.', 'Answering "Jesus ate fish" with a contested story accepts the other person\'s ground: that Jesus\'s diet would settle the question.'],
   lugar="What Jesus ate in first-century Galilee doesn't decide what we should do today. Christians follow his values, not his habits, and compassion for the vulnerable is at their center."),
 'Grandes gênios como Einstein e Da Vinci eram veganos.': dict(frase='Great geniuses like Einstein and Da Vinci were vegan.',
   por=["It's the same appeal to authority as the opposing argument, just from the other side. If the diet of famous thinkers doesn't prove that eating meat is right, it doesn't prove that not eating it is either.", 'And lists of "vegan geniuses" tend to contain errors: Einstein, for example, only became vegetarian near the end of his life, and there is no record that he was vegan. One error is enough for the whole list to lose credibility.'],
   lugar="What matters are the reasons, not who holds them. If you cite someone, cite the argument: Plutarch wrote against eating meat, and many contemporary philosophers defend vegetarianism or veganism as an ethical position."),
 'A ciência prova que o veganismo é o certo.': dict(frase='Science proves veganism is right.',
   por=['Science answers questions of fact, such as whether animals feel pain and whether a vegan diet can be healthy. Whether it is right to cause that suffering is a moral question, which no study answers on its own.', 'It is the same mistake as saying "veganism is opinion, not science", in the opposite direction: it confuses facts with values.'],
   lugar="Science shows that animals feel and that we can live well without exploiting them. Ethics, with its own methods, shows that causing unnecessary suffering is wrong, a principle almost everyone already accepts. It's the combination of the two that supports veganism."),
 'Numa ilha deserta, eu morreria de fome antes de comer um animal.': dict(frase="On a desert island, I'd starve before eating an animal.",
   por=['The very definition of veganism speaks of avoiding exploitation "as far as is possible and practicable". In a real survival situation, not eating an animal may be beyond what is possible, and saying otherwise creates a demand veganism does not make.', 'The phrase makes veganism look like fanaticism and steers the conversation toward a case no one lives.'],
   lugar="Maybe I would eat one, the way a starving person might steal food. That changes nothing in everyday life: none of us is on an island, and the question is what we do when we don't need to."),
 'Ostras e mexilhões sentem dor como qualquer outro animal.': dict(frase='Oysters and mussels feel pain like any other animal.',
   por=['The evidence of sentience in bivalves is weak: they have very simple nervous systems with no centralized brain, and even animal advocates admit they may not feel.', 'Claiming otherwise with certainty contradicts the evidence and weakens the cases where it is strong, like fish, octopuses and crabs.'],
   lugar="Maybe oysters and mussels don't feel, and the doubt justifies caution. But anyone who accepts this argument accepts that sentience is the criterion, and that criterion condemns eating the animals that do feel."),
 'Quem defende mudanças passo a passo não é vegano de verdade.': dict(frase="Anyone who supports step-by-step change isn't really vegan.",
   por=['Veganism is defined by refusing animal exploitation as far as is possible and practicable, not by a position on strategy. Someone can be vegan and support gradual reforms, or be vegan and reject them.', 'The debate between abolitionism and welfare reforms is legitimate, but it is an empirical question: what reduces the most suffering and brings the end of exploitation closer? That is answered with evidence, not by deciding who is really vegan.', 'Fights over who is "really vegan" push people away and use up energy that could go to the animals.'],
   lugar='Discuss strategy with evidence: which approach saves the most animals and most changes how people consider them. The question is what works, not who deserves the label.',
   nota="This doesn't mean every strategy, intervention and current is equally good for animals. There may well be a better path, and it should be sought in the evidence: what makes people consider animals, how to save more animals and which interventions work best. The evidence may favor one side. That doesn't stop people from different currents becoming vegan and then revising their moral and political views in other areas, seeking an even greater overall impact.",
   fontes=[('Sentience Institute: Foundational Questions Summaries', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries'), ('End Infighting', 'https://infighting.org'), ('The Vegan Society: definition of veganism', 'https://www.vegansociety.com/go-vegan/definition-veganism')]),
 'Quem não aceita mudar pouco a pouco não entende como o mundo funciona.': dict(frase="Anyone who won't accept gradual change doesn't understand how the world works.",
   por=['It mirrors the previous item, said by supporters of gradual change against those who call for an immediate end to exploitation. Asking people to stop now isn\'t naive: it states the goal clearly.', 'How the world changes is, again, an empirical question. Some people change gradually, others all at once, and different messages work with different audiences.', 'Defending gradual change as a strategy is different from saying it is the only legitimate path.'],
   lugar='Separate the goal from the strategy. The goal is that no animal is exploited; the path should be chosen by the evidence, and different people can contribute in different ways.',
   nota="This doesn't mean every strategy, intervention and current is equally good for animals. There may well be a better path, and it should be sought in the evidence: what makes people consider animals, how to save more animals and which interventions work best. The evidence may favor one side. That doesn't stop people from different currents becoming vegan and then revising their moral and political views in other areas, seeking an even greater overall impact.",
   fontes=[('Sentience Institute: Foundational Questions Summaries', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries'), ('End Infighting', 'https://infighting.org')]),
 'Quem compra produtos de grandes empresas não é vegano.': dict(frase="Anyone who buys products from big companies isn't vegan.",
   por=['Veganism is about the exploitation of animals. A vegan product from a big company involves no animal exploitation in the product itself. Criticisms of big companies can be legitimate, but they are a different discussion.', 'This demand makes veganism more expensive and harder, especially for those with less access, and pushes people away without saving a single animal.', 'Buying the vegan version from a company that also sells animal products can expand the vegan supply and signal demand. It is something to judge by its effect, not a purity test.'],
   lugar='If you have criticisms of a company, present them as criticisms of that company. For the animals, what counts is reducing demand for exploitation, and vegan products in ordinary stores usually help with that.',
   nota="This doesn't mean every strategy, intervention and current is equally good for animals. There may well be a better path, and it should be sought in the evidence: what makes people consider animals, how to save more animals and which interventions work best. The evidence may favor one side. That doesn't stop people from different currents becoming vegan and then revising their moral and political views in other areas, seeking an even greater overall impact.",
   fontes=[('Sentience Institute: Foundational Questions Summaries', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries'), ('The Vegan Society: definition of veganism', 'https://www.vegansociety.com/go-vegan/definition-veganism')]),
 'Quem é a favor do capitalismo não é vegano de verdade.': dict(frase="Anyone who supports capitalism isn't really vegan.",
   por=['It is the same mistake as demanding that vegans be anti-capitalist, in the opposite direction: it turns an economic position into a condition for defending animals.', 'There are vegans of many political positions. Economic systems and political currents may be better or worse for animals, and that is worth discussing with evidence, but not by expelling those who came to veganism by another route.', 'People from different currents can become vegan and later revise their views in other areas. Shutting them out at the door closes that path.'],
   lugar='Defend your economic position with its own arguments. For veganism, the question is whether the person has stopped exploiting animals; the rest is a conversation that can come later, and is worth having.',
   nota="This doesn't mean every strategy, intervention and current is equally good for animals. There may well be a better path, and it should be sought in the evidence: what makes people consider animals, how to save more animals and which interventions work best. The evidence may favor one side. That doesn't stop people from different currents becoming vegan and then revising their moral and political views in other areas, seeking an even greater overall impact.",
   fontes=[('Sentience Institute: Foundational Questions Summaries', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries'), ('End Infighting', 'https://infighting.org')]),
 'Veganismo e ambientalismo sem luta de classes é jardinagem.': dict(frase='Veganism and environmentalism without class struggle is just gardening.',
   por=['The phrase comes from a widely repeated saying, "ecology without class struggle is gardening", often attributed to the Brazilian activist Chico Mendes. Applied to veganism, it mixes two different causes: environmentalism does not presuppose veganism as a moral position. At most, for technical and evidential reasons, it recommends reducing animal farming because of its impact.', 'Veganism has another foundation: animals feel, and exploiting them is unjust. It depends neither on the environment nor on a theory of social classes, and it doesn\'t become "gardening" for lacking either.', 'It is the same move as demanding that vegans be anti-capitalist: it turns another agenda into a condition for defending animals. Class analysis can help in understanding the industry, but it isn\'t a prerequisite for no longer exploiting animals.'],
   lugar='Say what each cause stands for and why. If you think class struggle helps animals, show how, with evidence, without saying veganism without it is worthless.',
   nota="This doesn't mean every current is equally good for animals. There may be a better path, and it should be sought in the evidence. People from different currents can become vegan and later revise their views in other areas.",
   fontes=[('Sentience Institute: Foundational Questions Summaries', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries'), ('End Infighting', 'https://infighting.org')]),
 'Numa sala com um coelho e uma maçã, a criança come a maçã e brinca com o coelho.': dict(frase='In a room with a rabbit and an apple, a child eats the apple and plays with the rabbit.',
   por=["It's an appeal to nature: it suggests that what is right is what a child would do by instinct. Natural doesn't define right, and vegans point out this same mistake in \"it's natural to eat meat\". If children had the urge to bite the rabbit, that would not make it just to kill it.",
        "The fact also proves little. The child wouldn't kill the rabbit, but they wouldn't harvest wheat or peel a potato either: small children eat what adults prepare and put almost anything in their mouths. And the same child eats chicken nuggets without hesitation. What we eat is learned, and the scene does not show a \"natural diet\" of the species.",
        'The line swaps the ethical question for a dispute about instincts, which is the favorite ground of those who defend eating meat. It only takes a reminder that humans have hunted for hundreds of thousands of years for the argument to fall, and the animals stay in the same situation.',
        "The scenario is flawed, but the connection it suggests matters. The scene illustrates the concept of the absent referent, from the author Carol J. Adams: most of the time, the animal who became food is absent from the plate, hidden by words like \"steak\" and \"chicken\" and by packaging. Making the link between what we eat and the living animal is the central point. A viral 2017 video, shot by an Egyptian filmmaker in Saudi Arabia, shows two children, asked to prepare a chicken sandwich, who refuse to kill the live animal and cry. According to Fabio Chaves's investigation, no one knows what happened to the chicken, which probably met the fate of the others raised for consumption. If the scene is true, it shows that many people pay for an animal to be killed but would not have the courage to do it, and that the presence of the living animal awakens the refusal the plate hides. In other words, the discomfort with death exists, and habit and distance silence it. The rabbit line remains fallacious, because the reaction of children in a specific situation does not decide what is right, but that link deserves to be made."],
   lugar='Say what the research actually shows: young children value animals\' lives more than adults do, and many do not even know that meat comes from animals. The split between "animals to love" and "animals to eat" is taught. That does not prove what is right, but it shows that eating animals is a learned habit, and habits can be revised.',
   nota='In a 2021 study of 4- to 7-year-olds in the United States, many classified animal-based foods, such as bacon and hot dogs, as plants. In another, from 2022, children were less speciesist than adults.',
   fontes=[('Children are unsuspecting meat eaters: An opportunity to address climate change', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvp.2021.101705'), ('The development of speciesism: age-related differences in the moral view of animals', 'https://doi.org/10.1177/19485506221086182')]),
 'Não veganos são nossos inimigos, e odiá-los é normal.': dict(frase='Non-vegans are our enemies, and hating them is normal.',
   por=['Almost every vegan was once a non-vegan. People who eat animals today are, in general, where we used to be: repeating a habit learned in childhood, without ever having stopped to examine it. Treating them as enemies is treating who we were as an enemy.',
        'Hatred aims at people, and the problem lies in the practice. You can regard animal exploitation as a grave injustice and still recognize that those who take part in it are usually ordinary people, capable of affection and of change (E14).',
        'It is counterproductive. The people who still eat animals are the only ones who can stop: they are the audience, not the adversary. Someone who feels hated defends themselves instead of listening. And living angry at almost everyone wears advocates down and leads many to leave activism.',
        'Anger at what happens to animals is understandable, and feeling it is not a flaw. The mistake is turning the feeling into a rule, saying that hating is normal and aiming the hatred at people instead of aiming the energy at what needs to change.'],
   lugar='Aim your indignation at the practice and at those who organize it and profit from it, and treat each person as someone who can change, as you did. Firm with the argument, respectful with the person.',
   nota='Feeling anger at an injustice is human. What is at issue here is what you do with it: hating people helps no animal.'),

 'Todo vegano precisa ser amigável com os não veganos, ou não convencemos ninguém.': dict(frase="Every vegan has to be friendly with non-vegans, or we won't convince anyone.",
   por=["There is a lot of truth here: hostile conversations tend to produce defensiveness and rejection, and someone who feels attacked stops listening. Treating the person with respect helps.",
        "But \"it only works if it's friendly\" is an exaggeration. A very friendly approach risks being dismissed or ridiculed in some contexts, and the person may not grasp the seriousness of what is being discussed. Being friendly doesn't mean agreeing with everything, nor failing to say clearly what happens to the animals.",
        "Nor is it necessary to underestimate or look down on the intelligence of the listener: a patronizing tone puts people off as much as an aggressive one. There are contexts in which a firmer tone is right. The point is to find the tone that fits the person and the situation."],
   lugar='Combine respect with clarity: treat the person as someone able to think, say what happens to the animals plainly and adjust the tone to the context.',
   nota='How well each tone works is an empirical question, and it is worth looking for evidence rather than conviction.',
   fontes=[('Summary of Evidence for Foundational Questions in Effective Animal Advocacy', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries')]),
 'Precisamos confrontar os não veganos, já que eles é que são os extremistas.': dict(frase="We have to confront non-vegans, since they're the extremists.",
   por=["Part of the sentence has a basis: confronting an unjust practice is legitimate, and thinking it wrong to kill animals needlessly is not an extreme position.",
        "The problem is the method. A very aggressive approach can push away people who could become vegan and cause a backlash, in which the person digs into the opposite position. Labeling non-vegans extremists also swaps the argument for an attack.",
        "Confronting is not the same as attacking: you can be direct, firm and honest about the facts without humiliating anyone. In some cases, such as exposés and public campaigns, a harder stance may be used, but the context and the effect have to be weighed very carefully."],
   lugar='Be firm with the argument and respectful with the person. Keep the harder confrontation for situations where there is reason to expect it to help, and assess the results.',
   nota='This item and the previous one are opposites: neither tone works every time, and the most useful thing is to adjust the tone to the context and check the effect.',
   fontes=[('Summary of Evidence for Foundational Questions in Effective Animal Advocacy', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries')]),
 'Sou vegano, mas não tenho nada contra quem come carne.': dict(frase="I'm vegan, but I have nothing against people who eat meat.",
   por=["It is a very common line, said to avoid conflict, and the intention is good: respecting people. But it mixes two things, having nothing against the person and having nothing against what they do.",
        "If veganism is the refusal to exploit animals, it does have something against the practice. Saying otherwise turns it into a personal preference, as if it were a matter of taste, which weakens the message and contradicts the very reason for being vegan.",
        "You can say the same thing truthfully: \"I have nothing against you, but I am against what happens to the animals\". Respect for the person and disagreement about the practice can coexist."],
   lugar='Separate the person from the practice: "I respect you, and I disagree with eating animals, because they suffer and don\'t need to die".'),
 'Meu parceiro não é vegano, mas aceitamos nossas diferenças.': dict(frase="My partner isn't vegan, but we accept our differences.",
   por=["Respecting differences is important, and real relationships require care: no one has to break with someone they love over a disagreement, and there is no single formula. It is a controversial question.",
        "But the sentence can come from a speciesist view. If it were said about someone who harms people (\"we accept our differences, he assaults others\"), it would hardly be accepted. What changes is the victim, not the structure.",
        "Accepting differences and fighting injustice are not opposites. You can live together, talk, ask for changes and not fund exploitation at home, even with someone you love very much. What is questioned is pretending there is no injustice at stake."],
   lugar='Accept the person without treating the practice as neutral: talk, ask for possible changes, such as a home without animal products, and keep the bond.',
   nota='It is a controversial question: some argue for more flexibility in close relationships and others for more firmness.'),
}
